We have
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{1}{a}+\dfrac{1}{b} &= \dfrac{a+b}{ab} \\
&= \dfrac{a+b}{(a+b)-\dfrac{a^3+b^3}{ab}} \\
&= \dfrac{7}{7-\frac{42}{ab}} \\
&= \dfrac{1}{1-\dfrac{6}{ab}}.
\end{align*}
How do we express $ab$ in terms of $a$ and $b$, if it's any easier than we've already made it?
Not easy! It's the one which complicates the problem. Notice that
\begin{align*}
(a+b)^3 = a^3+b^3+3ab(a+b)
\end{align*}
and so
\begin{align*}
ab = \frac{(a+b)^3 - a^3 - b^3}{3(a+b)} = \frac{7^3-42}{3\cdot7} = \frac{28}{3}.
\end{align*}
Hrm, let's plug this value into what we've got so far.
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b} &= \frac{1}{1-\frac{6}{ab}} = \frac{1}{1-\frac{6}{\frac{28}{3}}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\frac{28}{28}-\frac{18}{28}} \\
&= \frac{14}{10} \\
&=\boxed{\frac{7}{5}}.
\end{align*}
